"alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-14-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1013024","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c370771124547aba558e757231966cc3484cc6d7b57cba3e725ec9e61f0ca3ed","compact":"8928c6442c7913c379a8506677097c2b2d19cd703207ef478db6ccad6ff1acdf","nasal":"8928c6442c7913c379a8506677097c2b2d19cd703207ef478db6ccad6ff1acdf"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-13-25","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":13,"verse":25,"english":"That fire-place of that lion among kings, set by skilful Brāhmaṇas, consisting on three sides of eighteen bricks, looked like the goldenwinged Garuda. And for the purpose of sacrificing them to the respective deities were collect beasts and reptiles, and birds, and horses, and aquatic animals. And the priests sacrificed all these in proper form. And to these Yūpas were bound, three hundred beasts, as well as the foremost of the best horses belonging to king Dasaratha.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.469,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-14-24","DUTT-1-14-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1013025","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b3f854bc92b397da1fbf8ab61bd0bc3996954de7e108c5f75310bc5ac3025ca4","compact":"7aceec89379a3e68e04823f271bd2f10f436b693e2a8b8517a358f59db438ba8","nasal":"c6bf27155cd4870948ff1bd2c276e6998c01d5131fa7f7a87545a8ae8fb8bb38"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-13-26","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":13,"verse":26,"english":"Then Kausalyā, having performed the preliminary rites, with three strokes slew that horse, experiencing great glee.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.804,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-14-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1013026","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprint